    "## Verify and validate your analysis: \n",
    "\n",
    "To verify and validate our analysis, we compared multiple independent estimates and checked for consistency across methods. First, we estimated the early exponential growth rate (𝑟) by fitting a line to the log-transformed case counts during the initial growth phase (days 20–100). From this, we derived an initial estimate of the basic reproduction number \n",
    "𝑅(0)=1+𝑟𝐷 assuming an infectious period D=5 days.\n",
    "\n",
    "We got a final 𝑅(0) of around 5.0 \n",
    "\n",
    "Next, we fit a full SEIR model using Euler’s method and performed a grid search over the parameters β, σ, and γ to minimize the sum of squared errors (SSE) between the model and observed data. The resulting best-fit parameters produced a trajectory that closely matched the observed epidemic curve, particularly capturing the rapid growth phase and the eventual peak and decline.\n",
    "\n",
    "We validated the model by comparing:\n",
    "\n",
    "The initial exponential growth prediction vs. the SEIR model fit\n",
    "\n",
    "The timing and magnitude of the infection peak\n",
    "\n",
    "The overall shape of the epidemic curve\n",
    "\n",
    "While the model aligned well with the general trend of the data, some deviations were observed, particularly in the later stages. These differences likely reflect real-world complexities not captured in the model, such as behavioral changes or reporting inconsistencies. Overall, the agreement between independent methods increases confidence that the model captures the core transmission dynamics"
   ]
