    "## Conclusions and Ethical Implications: \n",
    "Based on our model, we conclused that is was a novel, rebella-like togavirus. Another key conclusion from our model is the relative effectiveness of different interventions. The results suggest that the vaccination event had a greater impact on reducing transmission than the gradual vaccine rollout, likely because it rapidly decreased the susceptible population over a short time period. In contrast, slower rollout strategies may have allowed transmission to continue during the distribution period.\n",
    "\n",
    "Additionally, the school closure at Virginia Tech appears to have been more effective than the mask mandate in reducing case counts. This is consistent with the idea that reducing contact rates (through closures) has a stronger immediate effect on transmission than mitigation strategies like masking, which reduce, but do not eliminate the spread.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the model also shows that after the school reopened, there was a noticeable spike in cases, indicating that the underlying transmission risk remained. This suggests that while closures are effective in the short term, they may only delay infections unless combined with other interventions such as vaccination or sustained behavioral changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "These findings have important ethical implications. While interventions like school closures may be highly effective epidemiologically, they come with significant social and economic costs. Students may face disruptions to learning, mental health challenges, and unequal access to resources. Similarly, rapid vaccination events may be effective but raise concerns about equitable access, particularly for vulnerable or underserved populations."
   ]
