    "## Limitations and Future Work: \n",
    "Our analysis has several important limitations. First, the SEIR model assumes a homogeneous population, meaning all individuals have the same probability of interacting and transmitting the disease. In reality, populations are highly heterogeneous, with differences in behavior, contact patterns, and susceptibility. Also, the model assumes constant parameters over time. However, real-world factors such as policy interventions, behavioral changes, and seasonal effects can cause these parameters to vary. This may explain discrepancies between the model and observed data, particularly in later stages of the outbreak. One last limitation is that Euler’s method introduces numerical approximation error, especially with larger time steps, though we mitigated this by using a small step size.\n",
    "\n",
    "3 things that could improve future work: \n",
    "\n",
    "Use more advanced numerical methods for greater accuracy\n",
    "Incorporate time-varying parameters to reflect interventions or behavior changes\n",
    "Model population structure, such as age groups or contact networks"
